      },
      "text": "Cancún – Cobá – Tulum – Gran Cenote"
    },
    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:2e780f63f4172747",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "db5a6a514015ef8302c9ad21f7edc09306d1d00f0ff68ba6b78be53c46b21e3b",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-worker-d585b2821829f1fdbfcb5695",
        "model": "gpt-6.1-sol"
      },
      "text": "Breakfast.\n\nYour first stop is the Cobá archaeological site deep in the jungle. Along with Tikal, Cobá is considered one of the important ceremonial centres of the Classic Maya period.\n\nYou then visit Tulum. The site is relatively small and stands in an unusual setting: the ancient city sits on a cliff about 15 m above the turquoise waters of the Caribbean Sea. The beaches of the Riviera Maya are close to the ruins. Tulum means “wall” in Maya. The city was surrounded by a defensive wall, an unusual feature among the ancient cities of the region.\n\nAfter visiting the archaeological sites, you head to Gran Cenote. You can swim in the clear water and see natural formations in the underground water system."
    },
